<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanghamitta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">282–<a href="/years/203-bce">203 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Saṅghamittā, Sanghamitra, Sangamitta, Ayapali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanghamitta was an Indian Buddhist nun who, according to Sri Lankan tradition, was the eldest daughter of the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. With her brother Mahinda she entered the Buddhist order, and at the request of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanampiya-tissa">Devanampiya Tissa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> she was sent there with other nuns to ordain Anula and the women of the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, founding the Bhikkhuni Sangha, the order of nuns. She brought a sapling of the Bodhi tree, which was planted at Anuradhapura and still survives. Tradition holds that she died in 203 BCE at the age of 79. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhists in Sri Lanka mark her arrival every year on the full moon of December, Unduvapa Poya or Sanghamitta Day.</p></div>
